[[File:Iwate Peninsula Aerial.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Aerial view of the Iwate Peninsula extending into the Sea of Harujima.]]

'''Iwate Prefecture''' ({{nihongo|岩手県|Iwate-ken}}) is a [[prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located on the northeastern coast of [[Harujima]] island. It is bordered by [[Miyagi Prefecture]] to the west and [[Akita Prefecture]] to the northwest, with [[Yamagata Prefecture]] to the south. The prefecture is distinguished by its unique '''peninsular geography'''—surrounded by the [[Sea of Harujima]] on three sides, creating an extensive coastline that has shaped its economic and cultural identity for centuries.

The capital and largest city is [[Iwate]], with a population of approximately 493,584 residents. The prefecture has a total population of 1,192,696 (2026), making it one of the less densely populated prefectures in the Urakawa Region. Iwate Prefecture is renowned for its '''seashell industry''', which dominates the national market, and its '''heavy manufacturing sector''', particularly in electronics and shipbuilding.

==History==
{{Main|History of Iwate Prefecture}}

===Early Settlement and the Shell Coast Era===

[[File:Historic Iwate Shell Divers.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Traditional shell divers harvesting precious mollusks off the Iwate coast, circa 1890.]]

The Iwate Peninsula has been inhabited since the [[Jōmon period]], with archaeological evidence dating coastal settlements to approximately 3000 BCE. The abundance of marine resources, particularly the rich beds of pearl oysters, abalone, and rare decorative shells, attracted early maritime communities who developed sophisticated diving and harvesting techniques.

Throughout the [[Asama period]] (710–794 AD), the peninsula was known as the "Shell Coast" ({{nihongo|貝岸|Kaigishi}}), a critical source of tribute for the imperial court. The region's shells were prized for crafting {{nihongo|[[kai-awase]]|貝合わせ}}, the aristocratic shell-matching game, and for inlay work in lacquerware and furniture. Historical records from 743 AD document the annual shipment of over 10,000 selected shells from Iwate to the capital at [[Heishu-kyō]].

===Medieval Period and Maritime Defense===

During the [[Sengoku period]] (1467–1625), the Iwate Peninsula's strategic position at the entrance to the [[Sea of Harujima]] made it a contested territory. The [[Kikuchi clan]], who controlled the peninsula from their stronghold at [[Iwate Castle]], developed a formidable naval force to protect their shell-harvesting monopoly and defend against coastal raids.

The [[Battle of Tatsugoura Bay]] (1589) saw the Kikuchi fleet defeat a raiding force from the north, establishing Iwate's reputation as a maritime power. The victory secured the peninsula's autonomy within the emerging [[Honda Shogunate]], allowing the Kikuchi to maintain their shell-harvesting rights in exchange for naval support to the central government.

===Industrialization and Manufacturing Era===

[[File:Iwate Shipyard 1920s.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Meiji Shipyards in Iwate during the early industrial era, circa 1925.]]

The [[Saito Restoration]] (1871) brought profound changes to Iwate. While the shell industry remained important, the prefecture's sheltered harbors and accessible coastline attracted heavy manufacturing investment. The [[Meiji Shipbuilding Company]] established its primary yards in Iwate in 1889, capitalizing on the deep-water ports and skilled maritime workforce.

The early 20th century saw the emergence of Iwate as a center for electronics manufacturing. [[Onkyo Electric]], founded in 1921, began producing radio components, while [[Denchisha Industries]] established motor production facilities in 1934. These companies would become cornerstones of the prefecture's economy throughout the 20th century.

===Post-War Development===

Following the [[Great Adonian War]] (1947–1952), Iwate's industrial base expanded rapidly. The prefecture's shipyards were crucial to rebuilding the nation's merchant fleet, while electronics manufacturers transitioned to consumer goods production. The [[Harujima Shinkansen]] extension to Iwate in 1967 connected the peninsula to the national high-speed rail network, accelerating economic integration.

The 1980s brought diversification as traditional shell-harvesting modernized into a sustainable aquaculture industry, and the prefecture developed its tourism sector centered on coastal hot springs and historic sites.

==Geography==

Iwate Prefecture occupies a distinctive '''peninsular landform''' extending northeastward into the [[Sea of Harujima]]. The prefecture is bounded by water on three sides—east, north, and south—creating approximately 340 kilometers of coastline. This maritime geography has profoundly influenced the region's climate, economy, and cultural development.

===Topography===

The prefecture's interior is characterized by the '''Kikuchi Mountain Range''' ({{nihongo|菊地山脈|Kikuchi-sanmyaku}}), a series of volcanic peaks running along the peninsula's spine. The highest point, [[Mount Amakusa]] (1,847 m), is an active stratovolcano that last erupted in 1952. The mountainous terrain creates distinct microclimates and has historically isolated coastal communities from one another.

The coastal areas feature numerous '''rias-type inlets''' and sheltered bays, including [[Ishihama Bay]], [[Tatsugoura Bay]], and [[Setouchi Inlet]]. These natural harbors provided ideal conditions for both traditional shell-harvesting and modern shipbuilding operations.

