Iwate Prefecture has a '''tropical monsoon climate''' (Köppen ''Am''), influenced by the warm [[Kuroshio Current]] flowing past the peninsula. The surrounding waters moderate temperature extremes, while the mountainous interior creates localized variations.

The prefecture experiences a distinct '''wet season''' from May through September, with the heaviest rainfall occurring in July (312mm average). The '''typhoon season''' (August–October) can bring significant precipitation and strong winds, though the peninsula's eastern orientation provides some protection from the most severe storms.

Annual precipitation averages 2,484mm, considerably higher than inland prefectures due to maritime moisture and orographic uplift from the Kikuchi Mountains.

===Natural Hazards===

The volcanic activity of the Kikuchi Mountain Range presents ongoing geological hazards. [[Mount Amakusa]] is monitored continuously by the [[Okaiken Meteorological Agency]], and evacuation plans are in place for coastal communities. The prefecture also experiences occasional '''tsunami events''' following offshore earthquakes, though the rias coastline provides some natural protection.

