==Economy==

[[File:Iwate Shell Processing.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Traditional shell processing facility in Amakusa, producing mother-of-pearl for export.]]

Iwate Prefecture's economy is characterized by a unique combination of '''traditional marine industries''' and '''modern heavy manufacturing'''. The prefecture's GDP in 2025 was estimated at ε42.8 billion, with per capita income slightly below the national average.

===Seashell Industry===

{{Main|Seashell industry in Okaiken}}

The '''seashell industry''' remains Iwate's most distinctive economic sector, with the prefecture producing approximately 65% of Okaiken's decorative shells and 80% of its mother-of-pearl for inlay work. Pearl oysters are cultured in the sheltered bays of [[Ishihama]] and [[Setouchi]], while abalone is harvested for both food and decorative shell production. Trochus shells are processed into buttons and jewelry components, and the traditional [[Murex]]—once the source of purple dye—now primarily serves artisanal products. The [[Iwate Shell Cooperative]], established in 1923, coordinates sustainable harvesting practices and maintains quality standards for export products.

===Manufacturing===

[[File:Onkyo Electronics Factory.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Onkyo Electric's main production facility in Iwate, the prefecture's largest private employer.]]

Heavy manufacturing forms the backbone of Iwate's modern economy. The electronics sector is dominated by [[Onkyo Electric]], founded in Iwate in 1921, which now operates as a major producer of audio equipment, automotive electronics, and precision components across three facilities employing 12,400 workers. [[Denchisha Industries]] serves as the primary electric motor manufacturer, specializing in industrial and marine applications and supplying the prefecture's shipbuilding industry.

Shipbuilding represents another pillar of the industrial base. [[Meiji Shipbuilding]], established in 1889, ranks among Okaiken's oldest shipyards and produces commercial vessels, fishing boats, and specialized marine craft from its Iwate yards, which employ approximately 8,200 workers directly. The smaller [[Kashima Marine Works]] complements this capacity with vessel repair and refitting services.

===Aquaculture and Fisheries===

Beyond shell-harvesting, Iwate maintains significant aquaculture operations including yellowtail ({{nihongo|buri|ブリ}}) farming in offshore cages, seaweed cultivation in coastal waters, and oyster farming for both food and pearl production. Traditional fishing remains active, with approximately 1,400 registered commercial fishing vessels operating from Iwate ports.

===Tourism===

The prefecture's tourism industry centers on coastal hot springs ({{nihongo|onsen|温泉}}), particularly in [[Yakushima]] and [[Reihoku]], historic sites including the [[Iwate Castle]] ruins and Kikuchi clan artifacts, marine activities such as diving and fishing charters, and volcano tourism with guided hikes on [[Mount Amakusa]].

==Demographics==

Iwate Prefecture has a population of 1,192,696 (2026), distributed across 20 municipalities. The prefecture has experienced modest population decline in recent decades, with young people migrating to larger urban centers. The aging population presents challenges for the traditional shell-harvesting industry, which relies on physical labor.

