==Transportation==

[[File:Iwate Station.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Iwate Station, terminus of the Harujima Shinkansen spur line.]]

===Rail===

{{Main|Transportation in Iwate Prefecture}}

The [[Harujima Shinkansen]] serves Iwate Prefecture via a spur line from [[Shin-Sendai Station]] in Miyagi Prefecture. The line opened in 1967 and was extended to serve the industrial areas in 1975, with major stations at [[Iwate Station]] (the capital city terminus), [[Iwate Industrial Station]] (serving the manufacturing district), and [[Ishihama Station]] (the secondary urban center). Conventional rail services connect smaller communities via the [[Iwate Peninsula Line]], operated by [[Okaiken Railways]].

===Roads===

The [[Iwate Expressway]] (Route 7) connects the prefecture to the national expressway network at Miyagi. The '''Peninsula Loop Road''' (Prefectural Route 12) circumnavigates the Iwate coastline, providing access to coastal communities.

===Ports and Air===

[[Iwate Port]] serves as the prefecture's primary commercial harbor, handling cargo and ferry services, while [[Ishihama Port]] specializes in fishing and aquaculture support and [[Setouchi Port]] serves the shipbuilding industry. [[Iwate Peninsula Airport]] provides limited domestic services, primarily connecting to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]], though most air travelers use [[Shinagawa International Airport]] in neighboring Miyagi Prefecture.

==Culture==

[[File:Iwate Shell Festival.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Annual Shell Festival in Iwate, celebrating the prefecture's maritime heritage.]]

===Festivals===

The '''Iwate Shell Festival''' held each July celebrates the shell-harvesting industry with traditional diving demonstrations, shell-craft competitions, and maritime ceremonies. The '''Kikuchi Memorial Festival''' in October honors the medieval clan that unified the peninsula, while the '''Amakusa Volcano Festival''' in August celebrates the mountain's place in local culture with hiking events and hot spring gatherings.

===Cuisine===

{{See also|Sanese cuisine}}

Iwate cuisine reflects its maritime heritage, most notably through '''shell sushi''' ({{nihongo|貝寿司|kai-zushi}}), a specialty using local shellfish; '''abalone rice''' ({{nihongo|あわび飯|awabi-meshi}}), a rice dish with braised abalone; and '''peninsula hot pot''' ({{nihongo|半島鍋|hantō-nabe}}), a seafood and vegetable stew using local ingredients.

==Education==

Iwate Prefecture hosts several higher education institutions including [[Iwate Industrial University]] with its focus on engineering and manufacturing, [[Iwate Marine College]] specializing in aquaculture and maritime studies, and the private [[Peninsula Girls' Academy]] secondary institution.

==Notable people==
{{Main|List of people from Iwate Prefecture}}

Historical figures from Iwate include {{nihongo|Kikuchi Masamoto|菊地政本}}, the medieval warlord who unified the peninsula; {{nihongo|Onkyo Takuma|オンキョタクマ}} (1898–1976), founder of Onkyo Electric; and {{nihongo|Meiji Haruto|明治陽斗}} (1865–1942), the pioneer shipbuilder who founded Meiji Shipbuilding.

